    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the family court act, in relation to representation of adults\nand children in family court proceedings; and to amend the social services\nlaw, in relation to access by attorneys for adults and children to records of\nthe statewide registry of child abuse and maltreatment",
    "basePrintNo": "S9695",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9695-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd Art 2 Part 6 Part Head, §§261, 262 & 1094, add §1016-a, Fam Ct Act; amd §422, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Family Court Act",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9695\n\nSPONSOR: BRISPORT\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the family court act, in relation to representation of\nadults and children in family court proceedings; and to amend the social\nservices law, in relation to access by attorneys for adults and children\nto records of the statewide registry of child abuse and maltreatment\n\n2.  \nSOURCE OF BILL\n\nThis bill is being introduced at the request of the Judiciary, upon the\nrecommendation of the Chief Judge's Commission on Parental Legal Repre-\nsentation and the Chief Administrative Judge's Family Court Advisory and\nRules Committee.\n\n3.  \nPURPOSE OF BILL\n\nThis bill makes clear that there is a right to counsel during the inves-\ntigatory phase of a child protective proceeding and ensures that attor-\nneys for parents and children will have access to records necessary to\nenable the provision of representation prior to the filing of court\nproceedings.\n\n4.  \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends Family Court Act § 261 to replace the term\n\"indigent\" with \"adults who are financially unable to obtain counsel.\"\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends Family Court Act § 262 to require implemen-\ntation of Family Court Rules, including Rule 205.19 (22 NYCRR 205.19)\nregarding financial eligibility for assigned counsel, and to clarify\nthat representation provided prior to the filing of a petition is\nincluded in the appointment and is eligible for compensation.\n\nSection 3 of the bill adds Family Court Act § 1016-a to clarify the\nparents' right to counsel during the investigative phase of a child\nprotective proceeding, to require the courts to give notice of the right\nto counsel, to clarify the duration of the appointment of counsel, and\nto require that reports and other documents be furnished to counsel.\n\nSection 4 of the bill amends Family Court Act § 1094 to provide that\nrepresentation provided to a party prior to the filing of a petition\nregarding a destitute minor is included in the appointment and is eligi-\nble for compensation.\n\nSection 5 of the bill amends Social Services Law § 422 to provide\nprospective attorneys, both for parents and for children, with access to\nreports made to the Statewide Central Register of Child Abuse and\nMaltreatment to enable them to check for conflicts of interest that may\nprevent representation, to assist the attorneys in preparing for the\ninitial court proceedings, and where possible to provide representation\nprior to the filing of petitions.\n\n5.  \nJUSTIFICATION\n\nIn a series of public hearings statewide, the Chief Judge's Commission\non Parental Legal Representation (Hon. Karen Peters, Chair) heard testi-\nmony that all too often, parents are unrepresented at the critical first\ncourt appearance in child neglect and abuse proceedings in Family Court\nat which children may be removed from their care. Parents are only rare-\nly represented during the important investigative stage when inter-\nvention to provide services has been shown to prevent the need for court\naction altogether. The Commission issued an Interim Report in 2019, in\nwhich it concluded, inter alia:\n\n\"Without meaningful parental representation, Family Courts may lack the\ncomprehensive information and evidence needed to make reasoned determi-\nnations and render proper dispositional orders. Without State funding\nand oversight, attorneys lack all the resources necessary to deliver the\neffective assistance to which parents are constitutionally entitled.\"1\n\nAs in criminal cases, provision of counsel to parents who are facing\npossible removal of children from their care is constitutionally\nrequired, as it is a critical stage of the proceeding. See, e.g., Matter\nof Ella B., 30 N.Y.2d 352 (1972). Additionally, both Family Court Act §\n262 and Surrogate's Court Procedure Act § 407 provide for the appoint-\nment of counsel for parents who cannot afford to pay for counsel in\ncases in which their rights regarding their children are at stake.\nSection 205.19 of the Uniform Rules for the Family Court was promulgated\nin order to implement the Commission's recommendations both for repre-\nsentation at the critical investigative stage prior to the filing of a\nchild abuse or neglect petition, and for a presumption of a person's\neligibility for appointed counsel to apply at any court proceeding that\nmay result in the removal of a child from that person's care.2\n\nThis bill is essential to facilitate early access to counsel and to\nrequire implementation of Section 205.19 of the Uniform Family Court\nRules. Pre-petition representation in child protective and destitute\nminor proceedings would be considered part of the legal representation,\nand the Family Court rule regarding financial eligibility for such\nrepresentation would be applicable.  Additionally, the amendment to\nSocial Services Law § 422 is essential to facilitate timely represen-\ntation of both parents and their children even before a Family Court\nchild protective petition has been filed.\n\n6.  \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY\n\nNone. This is a new proposal.\n\n7.  \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS\n\nThis bill would have no meaningful State fiscal impact.\n\n8. Effective date\n\nThis bill would take effect on the ninetieth day after enactment.\n\n1 Commission on Parental Legal Representation, Interim Report to Chief\nJudge DiFiore (2019) at 6.  \nPARENTAL LEGAL REPRESENTATION (NVCOURTS.GOVD\n\n2 UNIFORM RULES OF THE FAMILY COURT, 22 N.Y.C.R.R. § 205.19.\n\n\n\n\n",
